-- title:
--   Reduction of an integral basis spans the reduced regular functions
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be an algebraically closed field, $F$ a field extension of $L$, and $f \in F$ such that $F$ is finite-dimensional and separable over the intermediate field $L(f)$ generated by $f$. Let $\iota$ be a finite index type and $y : \iota \to F$ a family with: $\#\iota = [F : L(f)]$; each $y_i$ lies in every valuation subring $V$ of $F$ that contains the image of $L$ and contains $f$; the only family of polynomials $c_i \in L[X]$ with $\sum_i c_i(f)\,y_i = 0$ is the zero family; and every $h \in F$ lying in all such valuation subrings $V$ can be written $h = \sum_i c_i(f)\,y_i$ with $c_i \in L[X]$. Then there is a finite set $S$ of nonzero elements of $L$ with the following property. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $L$ at which every $s \in S$ has valuation $1$, let $\kappa$ denote the residue field of $A$, let $Fb$ be a field extension of $\kappa$ (in the same universe as $F$), and let $R$ be a regular prolongation of $A$ to $F$ with residue field $Fb$: a valuation subring $R.\mathrm{integers}$ of $F$ together with a surjective ring homomorphism $\mathrm{res} : R.\mathrm{integers} \to Fb$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal, such that for $x \in L$ one has $x \in A$ iff the image of $x$ lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$, $\mathrm{res}$ agrees with $\kappa \to Fb$ on $A$, and every nonzero element of $F$ has an $L$-multiple lying in $R.\mathrm{integers}$ with nonzero residue. Suppose $f \in R.\mathrm{integers}$, its residue $\bar f$ is transcendental over $\kappa$, and $[Fb : \kappa(\bar f)] = [F : L(f)]$. Then every $y_i$ lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$, and for every $h \in Fb$ such that $w.\mathrm{ord}\,h \ge 0$ for every place $w$ of $Fb$ over $\kappa$ (a valuation subring of $Fb$, not all of $Fb$, containing the image of $\kappa$ and a principal ideal ring, with $\mathrm{ord}$ the negative logarithm of its associated adic valuation) with $w.\mathrm{ord}\,\bar f \ge 0$, there are polynomials $q_i \in \kappa[X]$ with $h = \sum_i q_i(\bar f)\, \mathrm{res}(y_i)$.
--
--   This is the spanning half of Deuring's reduction theory for a one-variable function field modulo a prime divisor of the constant field: away from a finite set of constants, the residues of an integral basis for a separating generator $f$ generate the functions on the reduced curve that are regular wherever $\bar f$ is. It feeds the companion statement combining this spanning property with the linear independence of the residues over $\kappa[\bar f]$.
